## Load required libraries
```{r setup, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE}
library(tidyverse)
library(broom)
```

## Read data
```{r message=FALSE}
data <- read_csv("insurance.csv")
```

## Quick structure check
```{r}
glimpse(data)
```

## Scatter plot with regression line 
Visualize the relationship between Age and Charges to check for linear trend and possible outliers.
```{r message=FALSE}
scatter_plot <- ggplot(data, aes(x = age, y = charges)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +  
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = TRUE) +    
  labs(
    x = "Age (Years)",
    y = "Insurance Charges (USD)",
    title = "Age vs Insurance Charges (Simple Linear Regression)"
  )
scatter_plot
```

The scatter plot shows a generally increasing trend between age and insurance charges. Some variability is observed,
  and a few points appear as potential outliers. Overall, a linear relationship seems plausible for modeling.

## Fit model
Fit a simple linear regression model to quantify how age affects 
insurance charges.
```{r}
model <- lm(charges ~ age, data = data)
```

## View model
```{r}
result <- tidy(model)
result %>% knitr::kable( caption = "Model Results")
```
The tidy summary confirms a significant positive effect of age on insurance charges.
Each additional year increases charges by approximately 258 USD. The model is simple, so R-squared remains low,
  indicating other factors also influence charges.

## Model Performance 
Check overall model fit including R-squared, adjusted R-squared, and F-statistic.
```{r}
model_perf <- glance(model)
model_perf %>% knitr::kable(caption = " Model Perfermance")
```

The F-test confirms the model is statistically significant (p < 0.001), but the R-squared is low (~9%),
  indicating age alone explains a small portion of variability in insurance charges.

## Model dignistics
Summarize the findings after fitting the model and checking assumptions.

## Linearity & homoscedasticity
Check linearity and constant variance by plotting residuals vs fitted values.
```{r}
resid_plot <- plot(model, which = 1)
```
Ideally, residuals should be randomly scattered around zero. Any pattern may indicate non-linearity or heteroscedasticity. 
  In this dataset, slight funneling is observed, suggesting variance increases with fitted charges.

## Normality of residuals
Assess whether residuals are approximately normally distributed using QQ plot.
```{r}
qq_reg_plot <- plot(model, which = 2)
```
Points should lie close to the diagonal line. Deviations at the tails indicate non-normality. Some deviation is observed,
  especially for high charges, which is common in skewed datasets.

## Scale-location
Examine if residuals have constant spread across fitted values.
```{r}
scale_loc_plot <- plot(model, which = 3)
```
A horizontal line with evenly spread points indicates homoscedasticity. Here, residuals spread slightly increases with fitted values,
  suggesting mild heteroscedasticity.

## Cook's distance
Identify influential points that may disproportionately affect the model.
```{r}
cooks_plot <- plot(model, which = 5)
```
Points above the reference line may be influential. Few high-charge observations stand out, but most points are below threshold,
  indicating minimal influence on the model coefficients.

## Conclusion
The simple linear regression model indicates a statistically significant positive relationship between age and insurance charges.
  Each additional year of age increases charges by approximately $258. However, the model explains only about 9% of the variability (R² ≈ 0.089),
  indicating other factors also contribute substantially. Diagnostic plots reveal slight deviations from linearity and normality, as well as mild heteroscedasticity,
  which is expected in real-world insurance data. Overall, the model provides a reasonable first approximation of the age-charge relationship and serves as
  a foundation for further multivariate modeling.
